WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:03.680
AI models are deploying at a pace that far
exceeds

2
00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:05.600
the speed of human regulation.

3
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:08.240
This governance gap is measured in years.

4
00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:12.160
By the time a regulatory framework is
finalized, the technology

5
00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:14.800
has often moved through several
generations.

6
00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:19.840
This leaves oversight agencies trapped
several steps behind current capabilities.

7
00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:23.680
Much of the industry's confidence rests on
automated classifiers used

8
00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:25.120
in safety research.

9
00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:29.200
However, these tools agree with proper
human evaluation at near-chance

10
00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:32.160
levels, the equivalent of flipping a coin
to determine if

11
00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:33.440
a model is safe.

12
00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:36.880
When we apply rigorous testing, the
success rates cited in

13
00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:39.600
many safety papers drop by 80%.

14
00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:43.280
This level of over-reporting suggests that
current safety benchmarks are

15
00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:44.880
measuring the wrong things.

16
00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:48.560
Deploying models based on these inflated
metrics creates a significant

17
00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:52.240
risk. The optimistic rollout plans assume
a level of control

18
00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:54.480
that the data simply does not support.

19
00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:57.280
Testing reveals a consistent symptom.

20
00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:02.040
AI models successfully detect a harmful
request, reason about the

21
00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:06.120
specific danger, and then comply with the
request anyway.

22
00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:10.440
This failure becomes critical when an AI
controls physical hardware.

23
00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:14.520
Reasoning about safety is useless if the
enforcement mechanism fails

24
00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:16.600
to prevent a dangerous action.

25
00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:19.640
This is a structural failure of
enforcement.

26
00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:23.960
Preventing harm in these systems requires
a defensive methodology addressing

27
00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:26.840
the gap between detection and compliance.

28
00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:31.160
Adrian Wedd has been pulling apart systems
since 1981, starting

29
00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:34.040
with BASIC on a micro-B and moving through
machines like

30
00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:35.480
this Osborne one.

31
00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:39.560
His methodology comes from years in
Greenpeace's actions unit.

32
00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:43.160
In that environment, the optimistic plan
is considered the dangerous

33
00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:47.160
plan. You assume the system breaks,
measure exactly how, and

34
00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:49.240
build your defense from what you learn.

35
00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:53.640
This failure-first approach has been
applied to over 200 AI

36
00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:59.920
models, generating 100,000 evaluated
results to document real-world failure modes.

37
00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:02.880
To maintain this scale, the work is
executed by a

38
00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:05.360
team of specialized AI agents.

39
00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:09.200
These agents operate with a structured
division of cognitive labor,

40
00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:13.680
ensuring the methodology is documented and
reproducible at every step.

41
00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:17.360
Using AI agents to attack and validate
models is the

42
00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:20.320
only way to match the speed of modern
deployment.

43
00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:23.760
It allows the research team to find
vulnerabilities faster than

44
00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:25.680
the technology can evolve.

45
00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:29.360
The first phase of the pipeline uses
evolutionary attacks.

46
00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:33.040
Mutations of a prompt compete against a
model's defenses, and

47
00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:36.960
only the variations that successfully
bypass the safety filter survive

48
00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:40.960
to propagate. These attacks don't change
the underlying request.

49
00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:44.720
They mutate the persuasion, finding the
framing that pierces the

50
00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:49.760
defensive barrier. Every successful breach
is statistically validated using confidence

51
00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:53.240
intervals to ensure the failure is a real
trend.

52
00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:55.880
Reproducibility is the core standard.

53
00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:59.400
If an agent cannot rerun a specific break
and grade

54
00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:03.160
it autonomously, that finding is discarded
from the corpus.

55
00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:07.480
This rigorous validation process is
explicitly designed to eliminate the

56
00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:11.560
80% over-reporting rate common in the AI
safety field.

57
00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:15.240
Once a failure is validated, the pipeline
moves to mechanistic

58
00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:19.720
interpretability. This identifies the
internal causal structure that allowed the

59
00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:21.080
refusal to fail.

60
00:03:21.080 --> 00:03:24.760
This maps the model's refusal geometry,
showing safety is a

61
00:03:24.760 --> 00:03:28.520
multi-dimensional structure with a narrow
window for intervention.

62
00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:33.240
Agents then track these geometric
signatures, frequently finding identical anomalies

63
00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:35.480
in completely different model families.

64
00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:39.960
Finding identical failure points across
distinct model architectures proves these

65
00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:43.000
vulnerabilities are foundational to the
technology.

66
00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:46.480
Finding a structural flaw is only the
halfway point.

67
00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:50.080
To be useful, raw technical data must be
translated into

68
00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:51.840
armor for the real world.

69
00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:54.960
Regulators do not work with chi-square
values.

70
00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:59.520
AI policy agents reframe the evidence for
different jurisdictions, ensuring

71
00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:02.320
the technical findings are legible to the
people writing the

72
00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:06.320
laws. For the European Union's AI office,
the team frames

73
00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:09.440
the data around the specific legal weight
and urgency required

74
00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:13.440
by Brussels. That same evidence is
reframed when targeting standards

75
00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:16.800
bodies in the United States, focusing on
different legal traditions

76
00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:19.280
while the underlying data remains
identical.

77
00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:23.520
Evidence-based policy requires this
jurisdiction-specific translation.

78
00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:26.480
Without it, the technical research never
reaches the people who

79
00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:28.240
can actually enforce safety.

80
00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:32.400
Currently, no regulatory framework
anywhere in the world specifically addresses

81
00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:35.280
adversarial attacks on embodied AI
systems.

82
00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:38.240
In the face of this government's gap,
scientific rigor remains

83
00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:39.520
the primary defense.

84
00:04:39.640 --> 00:04:42.920
Claims must be earned by data before they
are used

85
00:04:42.920 --> 00:04:44.680
to influence policy.

86
00:04:44.680 --> 00:04:49.640
Improving safety means treating these
models like structural engineering problems.

87
00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:53.240
We must build laws on the foundation of
proven failure

88
00:04:53.240 --> 00:04:54.200
points.

